    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li>The richest city of archaic <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ionia">Ionia</a>, at the mouth of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/buyuk-menderes-river">Maeander</a>, with its own oracle of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/apollo">Apollo</a> at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/didyma">Didyma</a>.</li><li>Milesian colonists founded dozens of settlements around the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/black-sea">Black Sea</a>, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thales-of-miletus">Thales</a> and his successors began Greek philosophy there.</li><li>Miletus led the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ionian-revolt">Ionian Revolt</a> against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/persia">Persia</a> and was sacked in 494 BCE, a disaster that led to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greco-persian-wars">Persian Wars</a>.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p>The Athenian playwright <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/phrynichus-tragic-poet">Phrynichus</a> was fined for staging <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/the-capture-of-miletus">The Capture of Miletus</a>, because the play reminded Athenians of the disaster to their Ionian kin.</p></aside>
    </section>

