    <div class="profile-section-block" id="foundation">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Foundation</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/codrus"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/codrus.jpg" alt="Codrus" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/codrus">Codrus</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Kodros-Schale.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Karl August Baumeister (nach Braun), Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Miletus stood on a peninsula near the mouth of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/buyuk-menderes-river">Maeander River</a> on the west coast of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/asia-minor">Asia Minor</a>, with several sheltered harbours opening onto the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latmian-gulf">Latmian Gulf</a>. The site was occupied long before the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greeks">Greeks</a> arrived: a settlement with strong Minoan connections existed there in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-bronze-age">Middle Bronze Age</a>, and a Mycenaean town, later fortified with a wall, followed in the fourteenth and thirteenth centuries BCE. Most scholars identify this town with Millawanda, or Milawata, named in Hittite texts as a base of the Ahhiyawa, probably a Mycenaean Greek power, although the identification is not certain. After the collapse of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bronze-age">Bronze Age</a> world, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ionians">Ionian Greeks</a> settled Miletus around 1000 BCE. Tradition named <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/neleus-son-of-codrus">Neleus</a>, a son of the Athenian king <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/codrus">Codrus</a>, as its founder, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/herodotus">Herodotus</a> reports that the first settlers killed the Carian men of the district and married their women, a story that reflects the mixing of Greek and Carian populations in the region. Miletus became one of the twelve cities of the Ionian League, which met at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/panionium">Panionion</a> on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mount-mycale">Mount Mycale</a>, and it controlled the oracle of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/apollo">Apollo</a> at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/didyma">Didyma</a>, about 16 kilometres to the south, joined to the city by a Sacred Way. By the seventh century BCE it was the largest and richest of the Ionian cities.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="socioeconomy">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Socioeconomy</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/naucratis"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/naucratis.jpg" alt="Naucratis" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/naucratis">Naucratis</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Plate_sphinx_BM_GR1965.9-30.705.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The prosperity of Miletus rested on commerce, manufacture and colonisation. Milesian wool and woollen cloth were prized across the Greek world, and the city's merchants traded with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lydia">Lydia</a> and the Anatolian interior, with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a> and with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/black-sea">Black Sea</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pliny-the-elder">Pliny the Elder</a> credited Miletus with founding some ninety colonies, a figure that is probably exaggerated but reflects a real and sustained movement. Milesians settled the shores of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hellespont">Hellespont</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sea-of-marmara">Propontis</a> and the Black Sea, founding Abydos, Cyzicus, Sinope, Histria, Apollonia Pontica, Olbia and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/panticapaeum">Panticapaeum</a>, and these colonies sent grain, fish, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/timber">timber</a> and slaves in return for wine, oil and manufactured goods. In Egypt, Milesians were among the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greeks">Greeks</a> trading at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/naucratis">Naucratis</a>, where they had their own sanctuary of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/apollo">Apollo</a>. Some of the earliest coins, small pieces of electrum stamped with a lion, may have been struck at Miletus in the late seventh or early sixth century BCE. Society was sharply divided between wealthy landowners and merchants and a large population of craftsmen and labourers; according to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/plutarch">Plutarch</a>, factions called the Ploutis, the party of the rich, and the Cheiromacha, the party of those who worked with their hands, fought bitterly for control. Religious life centred on Apollo, worshipped at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/didyma">Didyma</a> and in the city as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/apollo-delphinios">Apollo Delphinios</a>, whose <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guild">guild</a> of singers, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/molpoi">Molpoi</a>, stood at the heart of civic cult.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="military">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Military</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-lade"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/battle-of-lade.jpg" alt="Battle of Lade" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-lade">Battle of Lade</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Ionian_revolt_Battle_of_Lade.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Ionian_Revolt_Campaign_Map-fr.svg: Eric Gaba (Sting - fr:Sting) derivative work: MinisterForBadTimes (talk), CC BY-SA 3.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Miletus was above all a sea power, but it also held out on land against the kings of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lydia">Lydia</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/herodotus">Herodotus</a> relates that under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sadyattes">Sadyattes</a> and then <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alyattes">Alyattes</a> the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lydians">Lydians</a> invaded Milesian territory every year for eleven years, burning the crops, but could not take the city because Miletus controlled the sea; the tyrant <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thrasybulus-of-miletus">Thrasybulus</a> finally persuaded Alyattes to make peace by staging a show of plenty when a Lydian envoy arrived. When <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cyrus-the-great">Cyrus the Great</a> conquered Lydia in 546 BCE, Miletus alone among the Ionian cities obtained a treaty on the same terms it had held with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/croesus">Croesus</a>, and so escaped the Persian conquest that followed. Its great military venture was the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ionian-revolt">Ionian Revolt</a>. In 499 BCE <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aristagoras-of-miletus">Aristagoras</a>, who governed the city for the tyrant <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/histiaeus">Histiaeus</a>, failed in a Persian-backed attack on Naxos and then led the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ionians">Ionians</a> in rebellion; with Athenian and Eretrian help the rebels burned <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sardis">Sardis</a> in 498 BCE. In 494 BCE the Ionian fleet of 353 triremes, including 80 from Miletus, was defeated by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/persians">Persians</a> at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-lade">Battle of Lade</a>, an island just off the city, after most of the Samian squadron deserted. The Persians then besieged Miletus by land and sea and stormed it. In 334 BCE the city resisted <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexander-the-great">Alexander the Great</a> before falling to assault.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="governance">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Governance &amp; Law</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/periander"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/periander.jpg" alt="Periander" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/periander">Periander</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Periander_Pio-Clementino_Inv276.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Copy of Ktesilas, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>According to tradition Miletus was first ruled by kings descended from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/neleus-son-of-codrus">Neleus</a> and then by an aristocracy. From the later seventh century BCE it repeatedly fell under tyrants. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thrasybulus-of-miletus">Thrasybulus</a>, who ruled around 600 BCE, was the friend of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/periander">Periander of Corinth</a>, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/herodotus">Herodotus</a> tells how, when Periander's messenger asked him how best to rule, he silently walked through a field cutting off the tallest ears of grain. After his time Miletus suffered two generations of civil strife, which Herodotus says was ended by arbitrators from Paros who gave power to the citizens whose farms they had found best kept. Under Persian rule the city was governed by tyrants loyal to the king. The best known was <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/histiaeus">Histiaeus</a>, who guarded the bridge over the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/danube">Danube</a> during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dariuss-scythian-campaign">Scythian campaign</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/darius-i">Darius I</a> around 513 BCE, and his cousin and son-in-law <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aristagoras-of-miletus">Aristagoras</a>, who formally gave up the tyranny in 499 BCE to win support for the revolt. The chief religious official of the city, the head of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/molpoi">Molpoi</a>, served as its eponymous magistrate, later known as the <em>stephanephoros</em>, and an inscribed list of these officials survives from 525/524 BCE onward. After its refoundation Miletus belonged to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/delian-league">Delian League</a>, and an Athenian decree of about 450 BCE regulated its government, probably after a period of oligarchic unrest.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="impact">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Global &amp; Regional Impact</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/didyma"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/didyma.jpg" alt="Didyma" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/didyma">Didyma</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Didymaion_front_AvL.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">AlexanderVanLoon, CC BY-SA 4.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Miletus is often called the birthplace of Greek philosophy and science. In the sixth century BCE <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thales-of-miletus">Thales</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anaximander">Anaximander</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anaximenes">Anaximenes</a>, known together as the Milesian school, sought natural rather than mythological explanations of the world, naming water, the boundless (<em>apeiron</em>) and air respectively as the source of all things. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/herodotus">Herodotus</a> reports that Thales foretold the solar eclipse that halted a battle between the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lydians">Lydians</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/medes">Medes</a>, conventionally dated 585 BCE, and Anaximander was said to have drawn the first Greek map of the world. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hecataeus-of-miletus">Hecataeus of Miletus</a>, who advised against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ionian-revolt">Ionian Revolt</a>, wrote a description of the known world and a critical treatment of Greek genealogies, and was a forerunner of Herodotus. The sack of the city in 494 BCE shocked the Greek world: the Athenian dramatist <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/phrynichus-tragic-poet">Phrynichus</a> staged <em><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/the-capture-of-miletus">The Capture of Miletus</a></em> and was fined for reminding <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/athens">Athens</a> of a disaster to its kinsmen. When Miletus was rebuilt after 479 BCE it was laid out on a regular grid of streets, a plan long associated with the town planner <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hippodamus-of-miletus">Hippodamus of Miletus</a>, though his own part in it is debated. Its colonies spread Greek settlement around the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/black-sea">Black Sea</a>, and the Hellenistic temple of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/apollo">Apollo</a> at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/didyma">Didyma</a>, begun around 300 BCE and never finished, was among the largest Greek temples ever attempted.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="decline">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Decline &amp; Fall</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/herodotus"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/herodotus.jpg" alt="Herodotus" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/herodotus">Herodotus</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Marble_bust_of_Herodotos_MET_DT11742_(cropped).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">CC0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The Persian sack of 494 BCE ended the age of Milesian greatness. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/herodotus">Herodotus</a> says most of the men were killed, the women and children enslaved, the sanctuary at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/didyma">Didyma</a> plundered and burned, and the captives settled at Ampe near the mouth of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tigris">Tigris</a>. The city revived after the Greek victory at Mycale in 479 BCE, but it never regained its former standing. Within the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/delian-league">Delian League</a> it was a tribute-paying ally under close Athenian supervision, and its war with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/samo">Samos</a> over Priene in 440 BCE drew <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/athens">Athens</a> into a war against Samos. Miletus revolted from Athens in 412 BCE and became a Spartan base, and under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peace-of-antalcidas">King's Peace</a> of 387/386 BCE it returned to Persian rule, later under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hecatomnid-dynasty">Hecatomnid</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/satrapy">satraps</a> of Caria. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexander-the-great">Alexander the Great</a> took the city in 334 BCE. Under the Hellenistic kings Miletus was usually a nominally free city that passed between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/antigonus-i-monophthalmus">Antigonus</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lysimachus">Lysimachus</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ptolemaic-kingdom">Ptolemies</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seleucid-empire">Seleucids</a>; its citizens honoured <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/antiochus-ii-theos">Antiochus II</a> with the title <em>Theos</em>, god, for removing the tyrant Timarchus around 259 BCE. After <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/attalus-iii">Attalus III</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pergamon">Pergamon</a> bequeathed his kingdom to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> in 133 BCE, Miletus came under Roman supremacy in the province of Asia, organised by 129 BCE. The silting of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/buyuk-menderes-river">Maeander</a> later left the site far inland, and its ruins lie near the village of Balat in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/turkey">Turkey</a>.</p></div>
      </div>

    <p class="profile-summary" id="summary"><em>Miletus was the leading city of archaic <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ionia">Ionia</a>: a trading power that planted dozens of colonies around the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/black-sea">Black Sea</a>, the home of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thales-of-miletus">Thales</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anaximander">Anaximander</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anaximenes">Anaximenes</a>, and the instigator of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ionian-revolt">Ionian Revolt</a>, whose destruction by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/persia">Persia</a> in 494 BCE helped set off the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greco-persian-wars">Persian Wars</a>.</em></p>

